## 🔐 Git Workflow Rules

### **Protected Branches**
- `develop` — active development; **ALL PRs target `develop`**.
- `master` — production (App Store) releases ONLY. Never open a PR to `master` except for a production release.

**Direct commits to `develop`** are allowed only when ALL hold: you are on `develop`, you are NOT in a worktree (`git rev-parse --git-dir` prints `.git`), and the change is minor (docs, config, RELNOTES, small fix). Otherwise use a branch + PR.

### **🌳 Git Worktree Rules**
In a worktree (created by `kb-run` or `git worktree add`; `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` is not `.git`, or the path contains `worktrees/`):
- The worktree's branch (e.g. `feature/xaca-0015`) IS the working branch. Commit there. Never create a "more descriptive" branch.
- 🚨 **Do not switch branches, `git checkout develop`/`master`, or create, edit or commit files on `develop`.** A worktree that touches `develop` corrupts every other worktree and the main repo, so no change is minor enough to justify it.
- **Run this before the first file operation of every task:**
```bash
B=$(git branch --show-current); if [ "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" != ".git" ] && { [ "$B" = develop ] || [ "$B" = master ]; }; then echo "🚨 STOP: on $B inside a worktree"; else echo "✅ $B"; fi
```
- If it prints STOP, stop all work. Don't try to fix it (no checkout, no stash). Report to the user and wait.

### **🤖 Subagents (Agent tool)**
Every subagent prompt needs:
1. **Worktree anchoring** (when you are in one): the exact directory, the branch name, and "Do NOT switch branches, do NOT checkout develop, all changes stay in this directory". Subagents inherit no git context.
2. **Crew-tracking cleanup as the LAST instruction.** Background subagents fire no completion hook, so their LCARS avatar otherwise persists forever:
```
MANDATORY FINAL STEP: Before completing, you MUST run this Bash command to remove your crew avatar from the LCARS tracking panel. This is required infrastructure cleanup, not a team-boundary violation:
python3 {{AITEAMFORGE_DIR}}/kanban-hooks/subagent-track.py remove <AGENT_TYPE> < /dev/null
```
`<AGENT_TYPE>` = the exact `subagent_type` you passed.

### **🎛️ Multi-Ticket Work: Orchestrate, Never Accumulate**
Context is re-sent every turn, so a session that works ticket after ticket pays for every earlier ticket on every later turn.
- **When asked to run more than one ticket, your session is a COORDINATOR only.** Launch each ticket's full run cycle in its own fresh session: `kb-run <REL-ID> --orchestrate` for a release, `kb-run <ID>,<ID>` for any other set (no terminal: run either with `--plan-only`, show the user the plan, and once they approve run the `--approve-plan` line it prints); otherwise `kb-run <ITEM-ID> --yes` per ticket. Do not implement, gate or merge the tickets in the coordinator's own context.
- The coordinator tracks status, answers `kb-ask` questions and escalates user decisions. Workers own their ticket through merge and `kb-done`.

### **Standard Development Workflow**

#### 1. Start New Feature/Fix
Main repo only (in a worktree you are already on your branch): `git checkout develop && git pull`, then `git checkout -b <prefix>/descriptive-name`.
Prefixes: `feature/`, `bugfix/`, `refactor/`, `hotfix/` (urgent production), `chore/`.

#### 2. Make Changes & Commit
Stage specific files and commit with a descriptive message (see Commit Message Guidelines).

#### 3. Push Branch
`git push -u <remote> <branch>`

#### 4. Create Pull Request
```bash
gh pr create --base develop --title "Feature: Clear descriptive title" --body "<description>" --assignee ehlersd
```
Then run `kb-pr` (moves the item to In Review), launch the gates and run the monitor (step 5).

#### 5. PR Review Handoff & Monitoring

All agents share one GitHub account, so `gh pr review` cannot approve. Two **different** GitHub Apps submit the formal reviews:

| Gate | Bot login | Submitted via | Covers |
|---|---|---|---|
| Test | `ds9-tester-bot[bot]` | `gh-bot-test` | Build / lint / test |
| Review | `ai-security-review-bot[bot]` | `gh-bot-review` | Security, architecture, quality |

- **Filter reviews by exact `.user.login`, never `.user.type`.** Both are Bots, so a type filter lets one gate's APPROVE satisfy the other.
- Pass `--repo owner/repo` to both bot commands whenever your remote is not named `origin`. Without it they exit 1 with "Could not auto-detect repository".
- Capture a command's own exit code before piping it. `cmd | tail` reports the pipe's status, so a failed submission can read as success.

Every PR passes **Test**, **Review**, the **protected-subitem sweep** (`kb-sweep` shows no `PROTECTED SUBITEMS UNRESOLVED`) and **branch freshness / mergeability**. The shipped script enforces all four. Run it; never hand-roll a polling loop.

**A. Launch the gates YOURSELF — never hand the user paste-in prompts**
```bash
kb-run-test <ITEM-ID> --yes      # TEST gate session (posts via gh-bot-test)
kb-run-review <ITEM-ID> --yes    # REVIEW gate session (posts via gh-bot-review)
```
If the diff touches UI, also run a UX gate: `kb-run-ux <ITEM-ID> --yes` where your install ships it, otherwise spawn the `lal` UX `Agent` gate and say so.
Launch them in parallel, in one message. They carry the context, checklist, finding budget and the DO-NOT-MERGE rule. Never also spawn `Agent` gates (double-spawn). Without `--yes`, a non-tty launch refuses with rc 2. Spawn `Agent` gates (thok tester / reed reviewer) ONLY if a launcher refuses, and say so.

**B. Run the monitor**
```
{{AITEAMFORGE_DIR}}/scripts/kb-pr-monitor --pr <N> --item <ITEM-ID> [--repo <owner>/<repo>] [--once] [--no-merge] [--merge-method squash|merge|rebase]
  sync PR (integration<->release): --merge-method merge, never squash
  0 GATES_PASSED (merged via kb-merge; caller still runs kb-done) — 0 is ALSO HELP (--help): read the NAME, not just $?
 10 RESPAWN_TESTER   11 RESPAWN_REVIEWER   12 RESPAWN_BOTH (update-branch ran)   13 STALE_APPROVAL
 20 CONFLICT   21 UX_REOPEN   22 BLOCKED   30 PR_CLOSED   31 TIMEOUT   2 usage/env
final stdout line: VERDICT: <NAME> pr=<N> item=<ID> detail=<...>
```
- **0** — already merged; run `kb-done`.
- **10 / 11** — push fixes, re-launch the named gate in full AND the other gate per its `*_mode=` hint (`kb-run-test` / `kb-run-review <ITEM-ID> [--delta <sha>] --yes`), re-run the monitor.
- **13** — a gate's only review is on an older commit. Any push after an APPROVE (e.g. fixing an advisory) makes it stale. No fix push: re-launch each `stale_bots=` gate per its `*_mode=` hint (`current` = leave it), re-run.
- **12** — `gh pr update-branch` ran, so both approvals are stale: re-launch BOTH gates, re-run.
- **20** — resolve by hand (merge the base in, fix, push); `update-branch` does not fix content conflicts. `CHANGELOG.md`: **union-resolve, keep BOTH blocks**, then count occurrences and drop genuine duplicates. Re-launch BOTH gates, re-run.
- **21** — the diff touches UI but `[UX]` was cancelled: re-open it, get a UX evaluation, re-run.
- **22** — a gate is red or unclassifiable: read `detail=` and investigate. Never `--admin` past it.
- **30 / 31 / 2** — stop and report to the user.

**Invariants**
- **Whoever runs a gate must NOT merge.** The creating agent merges after the monitor returns 0.
- **Finding budget: MAX 3** non-blocking `[Review]`/`[Test]`/`[UX]` subitems per round, highest severity first; the rest go in the review body under `### Additional observations (not filed)`. Blocking findings (security, data loss, breaking change without migration, missing error handling) are never capped.
- **Never read `reviewDecision`.** The Team plan ignores bot reviews, so it always says `REVIEW_REQUIRED`, and `BLOCKED` is the normal steady state; that is why merges use `--admin`. Measure staleness by counting commits, never by `mergeStateStatus`.
- A rebase or force-push can re-point an old review's `commit_id` onto the new head. Treat any rewrite as a fresh round and re-launch BOTH gates. The monitor's guards and their known gaps are documented in `kb-pr-monitor`'s own comments.
- The monitor's `sync-tap-drift` / `tap-changelog-completeness` arms are dev-team-only; in a `BLOCKED` verdict they mean you are in the framework's own repo.

